<p>Russia&#x27;s Defense Ministry said President Vladimir Putin ordered a unilateral ceasefire in Ukraine for May 8 and 9, coinciding with Victory Day commemorations of Nazi Germany&#x27;s defeat in World War II. The ministry said it expected Kyiv to observe the pause. It also warned that any Ukrainian attempt to disrupt the celebrations would trigger a “massive missile strike” on central Kyiv and told civilians and foreign diplomats to leave the city promptly. Ukraine rejected Moscow&#x27;s timing. President Volodymyr Zelensky said Kyiv had received no official request spelling out the terms of a halt in fighting and called a short-term halt meaningless. Ukrainian officials also said it was “not serious” to expect Ukraine to observe a ceasefire during a Russian military holiday. Zelensky announced that Ukraine would observe its own pause from 00:00 on May 6 and respond in kind to Russia&#x27;s actions from that point. The separate announcements followed Russian strikes that Ukrainian officials said killed nine people across Ukraine on Monday and a Ukrainian drone crash into a high-rise building in Moscow overnight.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/onZ__KvszUffu6Rx0hxX90Ve60HKjNSjCTLPZu_P8KMSK6oiPu1hVkRWlMlLQBc_ASdQWtGhtRQALyDHpJwSdAuh_4neDlApdArXLdmbyYKcGWCrMXSJO5kvJOB48WIdCC5MLmdPZs66oa9wcKXaBnBjKB8fEYmwpfs8us3UndAZF06O' alt='Russian servicemen await the rehearsal of the Victory Day military parade, to be held at Red Square, in central Moscow, May 4, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Parade security: Russia prepared a Red Square parade that officials said was scaled back because of possible Ukrainian attacks, and its Defense Ministry said no military hardware would appear this year because of fears of long-range drones.</li><li>Diplomacy lull: Dawn reported that the dispute over the truces occurred as US-led efforts to end the war had slowed, with Washington shifting attention toward conflict in the Middle East.</li></ul><blockquote>Drones may buzz 